00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:58.300
Imagine you bought a rate sensitive ETF last week expecting the next inflation print to confirm cooling.

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:06.140
Your order filled cleanly, yet the market reaction the next morning hinged on whether one data point was treated as signal or noise.

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:16.660
The mechanism is straightforward because monthly CPI prints carry both trend information and one-off distortions from tariffs, seasonality, or energy spikes.

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:25.860
A single hotter than expected number can shift Fed funds futures pricing by 10-15 basis points even when the six-month trend remains lower.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:34.200
What most retail investors do not realize is that policy makers explicitly weigh the cumulative run of misses against the latest release.

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:43.140
The pro tip is to check the Cleveland Fed's nowcast and the NY Fed's multi-variate core trend measure the same morning the CPI drops.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:49.060
Those models strip out in the volatile components and tell you whether the headline move is likely to stick.

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:54.600
The concrete misconception to avoid is treating every monthly deviation as a new regime.

00:03:55.140 --> 00:04:01.280
Instead, require at least two consecutive prints in the same direction before repositioning rate sensitive holdings.

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:16.020
This distinction matters for Canadian investors because Bank of Canada decisions often follow the same cumulative logic and rate sensitive holdings such as utilities or REITs in a TFSA can move sharply on even one misinterpreted print.

